      <methodStep>
        <description>
          <section>
            <title>General description</title>
            <para>
      The Jornada Basin LTER aboveground NPP study began in 1989 to
      examine variation in vegetation productivity over time in response
      to a variety of environmental drivers. The 15 NPP sites were
      selected to represent the 5 major ecosystem types in the
      Chihuahuan Desert (upland grasslands, playa grasslands,
      mesquite-dominated shrublands, creosotebush-dominated shrublands,
      tarbush-dominated shrublands). For each ecosystem type, three
      sites were selected to represent the range in variability in
      production and plant diversity; thus the locations are not
      replicates.
    </para>
            <para>
      In this data package, precipitation data collected in millimeters
      at all 15 NPP sites was gap-filled using available weather
      stations in the Jornada Basin. The principal purpose is to provide
      daily precipitation at these sites to assist the study of
      vegetation productivity responses to water availability.
    </para>
          </section>
          <section>
            <title>Weather station history and gapfilling</title>
            <para>
      The 15 NPP sites have been instrumented with graduated rain gauges
      where monthly rainfall observations have been collected since
      approximately 1989. Other sites around the Jornada basin have used
      a variety of rain gauge types to collect rainfall data on daily,
      monthly, or irregular intervals. To create a daily rainfall data
      product for the 15 NPP sites from 1980 to the present, data have
      been combined from many rain gauges based on spatial and temporal
      proximity to the NPP sites. In general, available daily
      precipitation at the NPP sites were gapfilled using the closest
      rain gauges to each NPP site that provided a minimum resolution of
      daily precipitation data. This selection and gapfilling algorithm
      was used up until approximately 2013 and the additional documents
      included with this dataset describe the process in much greater
      detail.
    </para>
            <para>
      In 2013, the 15 NPP sites were re-instrumented with networked,
      automated weather stations. This has led to significantly fewer
      missing rainfall observations since thi time. Currently, missing
      data at NPP sites are limited to battery failures or site visits,
      and these can be filled from the nearest or most similar automated
      station at another NPP site. This process is also described in
      additional documents attached to the dataset.
    </para>
          </section>
          <section>
            <title>Additional documents</title>
            <para>
      The attached "Method_gapfill_daily_precipitation.pdf"
      file describes the detailed methods used to gap-fill
      precipitation. The "daily_gapfill_ppt_gauge_usage.csv"
      file describes the source rain gauges, including their location,
      time period, and priority rank, used to gapfill NPP stations data
      until automated weather stations were installed in ~2013. The
      "site_edi_pkg_versions.csv" file lists data sources used
      to gapfill NPP sites after 2013. These sources correspond to
      datasets published to the EDI data repository from the automated,
      networked stations installed at NPP sites in 2013.
    </para>
          </section>
        </description>
      </methodStep>
